// deep_recursion.lox — Q3's own measured test (notes/qbe-backend.md):
// "1040 small frames fit easily in an 8 MB C stack, but ... needs a
// measured test." Every Lox call compiled code makes is a real C call
// (rt_call -> Runtime::call -> the compiled callee's own `call` back into
// rt_call for its own recursive call), unlike native's own VM::run(),
// which recurses the C stack only for defer/class-init, never for an
// ordinary CALL. No try/catch here (PUSH_HANDLER is S6's job, #459) — this
// probe instead drives plain, uncaught recursion well past native's own
// frame-count ceiling (kFramesMax, vm_limits.h) and checks that: (a) the
// process does not crash (the C stack holds — Q3's own question), and (b)
// it fails with the same stdout (empty), exit status, and "Stack
// overflow." diagnostic as native's own uncaught StackOverflowError.
fun count(n) {
  if (n < 1) return 0;
  return 1 + count(n - 1);
}
print count(2000);
